<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Uzbek Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1428 CE – 1471 CE (approx.) · capital Sighnaq</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The nomadic khanate of Abu'l-Khayr Khan on the Kazakh steppe, from which both the Uzbek dynasties of Transoxiana and the Kazakhs emerged.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1428 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shibanid</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr</a> was proclaimed khan in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> by the tribes of the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ulus-of-shiban">Ulus of Shiban</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/white-horde">White Horde</a>. He conquered the steppe as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and in 1446 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sighnaq">Sighnaq</a> and other Syr Darya towns, making them his base.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr</a> ruled a confederation of Turkic tribes, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qungrat">Qungrat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghud">Mangit</a> and Kiyat, known collectively as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>. He intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid</a> politics, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Urgench</a> in 1431 and backing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza">Timurid Abu Sa'id</a>. In 1457 his army was badly defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, and discontented clans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerei-khan">Kerei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janibek-khan">Janibek</a> broke away to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr</a> died in 1468 and his state collapsed amid rival claims, its tribes passing largely to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> regrouped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and in 1500 conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uzbek-khanate.jpg" alt="Uzbek Khanate" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: Ktrinko, Incall (Supplemented) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Uzbek_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1428 CE – 1471 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sighnaq</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu'l-Khayr Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kipchak Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Abu'l-Khayr, Abulkhairid Khanate, Nomadic Uzbek state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1450&amp;focus=uzbek-khanate" aria-label="Uzbek Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6089 240 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uzbek Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6409 496L6600 1003M6409 496L6409 496M6409 496L6409 496M6409 496L6588 1010M6409 496L6645 897"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6600 1003h0M6409 496h0M6409 496h0M6588 1010h0M6645 897h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6409 496h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6409 496h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1450&amp;focus=uzbek-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
